<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afyonkarahisar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Afyon, Karahisar-i Sahib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afyonkarahisar is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, capital of Afyonkarahisar province, beneath a volcanic rock crowned by a castle. In the late 13th century it was held by the family of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahib-ata-fakhr-al-din-ali">Sahib Ata</a>, after whom it was long called Karahisar-i Sahib, and it later passed to the Germiyanids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Its name refers to the opium poppy grown in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afyonkarahisar.jpg" alt="Afyonkarahisar" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ingeborg Simon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgberg_Afyonkarahisar_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Afyonkarahisar lies in the mountainous country inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> coast, about 250 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> on the Akarçay River. Its name means opium black fortress in Turkish, since opium was widely grown there and a castle stands on a black rock. The city was called Afyon until the Turkish Parliament changed the name to Afyonkarahisar in 2004. In antiquity it was known as Akroinon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The top of the rock has been fortified for a long time, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> knew it as Hapanuwa. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a>, then by the Achaemenid Persians until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered it. It was later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. Akroinon became an important fortress in the Armeniakon theme and was first mentioned in Byzantine history when Arab invaders attacked it in 716 and 732. After his victory over Arab besiegers in 740, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Emperor Leo III</a> renamed it Nicopolis, city of victory.</p><p>After 1071 the town lay in the frontier zone between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the invading Turks. It was still Byzantine in 1112, but it had been lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> by 1146, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> won a victory there. The Turks could not firmly control it until about 1210, and they renamed it Kara Hissar, black castle. After the dispersal of the Seljuqs the town was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahib-ata-fakhr-al-din-ali">Sahib Ata</a> and then by the Germiyanids. The Ottoman Sultan Beyazid I conquered the castle in 1392, it was lost in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1402, and it was recaptured in 1428 or 1429.</p><p>Lying on the caravan route between Smyrna and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, it became a place of extensive trade and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> a wealthy centre of opium production. A fire in 1902 burned for 32 hours and destroyed parts of the city. During the First World War British prisoners captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> were held in an empty Armenian church at the foot of the rock. Greek forces occupied the city in the Greco-Turkish War, and it was recovered on 27 August 1922, a key moment in the Turkish counter-attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Afyonkarahisar is the administrative centre of its province and a junction of railway and road routes between İzmir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. It has hot springs and spas, and it is known for its marble: Docimaean marble from the region, including the purple-veined Pavonazzetto, was valued by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and used under Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Hadrian for buildings such as the Pantheon. Raw opium production ended in the late 1960s under international pressure, and poppies are now grown under licence for morphine and other opiates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afyonkarahisar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1270s his sons were granted <b>Karahisar</b>, Kutahya and nearby lands, which the family held as a semi-independent domain after Seljuk authority collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/sahib-ataids">Sahib Ataids</a></li></ul></section>
